  "articleBody": "If your current kitchen feels cramped or cuts you off from the rest of the house, shifting the layout is usually the best answer. But moving key fixtures changes everything behind the drywall. Before you begin taking down partitions, take time to evaluate how the new traffic flow interacts with your home's structural framing and utility runs.\n\nWhat Can Change the Right Choice\n- Moving the Sink or Range: Shifting a sink means re-routing drain lines, vents, and supply lines. Moving a gas range or electric cooktop requires extending gas pipes or pulling new dedicated circuits.\n- Wall Removal: Opening up a kitchen to the living room looks great, but removing load-bearing framing requires engineered beams and specific permit drawings.\n- Island Clearances: Adding an island requires comfortable walkways between counters, plus code-compliant power outlets built right into the cabinetry.\n- Permit Requirements: Most jurisdictions require detailed plans showing both existing and proposed conditions for plumbing, electrical, and mechanical adjustments before work begins.\n\nHaving a clear plan prevents costly change orders after rough framing starts. Abraham Becerra and the crew at HH Remodeling LLC handle framing, drywall, and trade coordination across the Denver metro area. Call 303-505-0866 or email Hhremodelingllc1986.ab@gmail.com to discuss your kitchen floor plan.",
  "datePublished": "2026-10-23"
